WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.549
[MUSIC]

00:00:04.549 --> 00:00:06.069
I want to start with a big thank you.

00:00:06.069 --> 00:00:09.990
Thank you for deciding to
listen to this podcast episode.

00:00:09.990 --> 00:00:13.573
More important and relevant to our
topic at hand I want you to think about

00:00:13.573 --> 00:00:17.750
the other decisions you made throughout
your day prior to listening in.

00:00:17.750 --> 00:00:22.357
Chances are you've made many,
many decisions, some big, some small,

00:00:22.357 --> 00:00:25.570
some personal and some business related.

00:00:25.570 --> 00:00:30.050
Today we're going to discuss how
we can make better decisions.

00:00:30.050 --> 00:00:33.110
I'm Matt Abrahams and
I teach Strategic Communication at

00:00:33.110 --> 00:00:35.550
Stanford Heard Graduate School
of Business.

00:00:35.550 --> 00:00:37.929
Welcome to Think Fast Talk Smart
the podcast

00:00:37.929 --> 00:00:42.099
[MUSIC]

00:00:42.099 --> 00:00:46.158
I am really excited today to be
joined by both Jonathan Burke and

00:00:46.158 --> 00:00:47.698
Jules van Binsbergen.

00:00:47.698 --> 00:00:52.355
Jonathan is the AP Giannini Professor
of Finance at the GSB.

00:00:52.355 --> 00:00:56.365
Jules is the Nippon Life Professor
in Finance at Wharton.

00:00:56.365 --> 00:01:01.895
Together, Jonathan and Jules host
a new podcast entitled All Else Equal.

00:01:01.895 --> 00:01:05.345
I've listened in, and I've learned a lot.

00:01:05.345 --> 00:01:07.282
Thanks for being here, Jonathan and Jules.

00:01:07.282 --> 00:01:10.155
&gt;&gt; It's great to be with you, Matt,
thank you so much for having us.

00:01:10.155 --> 00:01:11.965
&gt;&gt; It's an honor to be here.

00:01:11.965 --> 00:01:14.295
&gt;&gt; Great well, thank you both.

00:01:14.295 --> 00:01:16.059
Let's get started.

00:01:16.059 --> 00:01:19.953
So Jonathan, you and Jules have
worked together for a long while and

00:01:19.953 --> 00:01:21.942
published many papers together.

00:01:21.942 --> 00:01:27.649
I'm curious what led you to podcasting and
what is the focus of your podcast?

00:01:27.649 --> 00:01:34.280
&gt;&gt; Getting that child, I podcast is
a lot making good business decisions.

00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:37.740
We both feel that people make
common mistakes repeatedly.

00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:43.193
And often what makes this worse is that
have no idea they're making a mistake.

00:01:43.193 --> 00:01:48.103
At the same time we hear business gurus
providing advice often either flat

00:01:48.103 --> 00:01:49.450
out wrong.

00:01:49.450 --> 00:01:52.820
On platitudes,
like treat your employees well.

00:01:52.820 --> 00:01:54.630
We thought we could just do better.

00:01:54.630 --> 00:01:58.510
We could provide substantive
advance based on research.

00:01:58.510 --> 00:02:02.738
In fact, in each episode, we will start
with a business decision that people

00:02:02.738 --> 00:02:05.940
make that they feel is obviously correct.

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:10.004
Will explain why it is a mistake and
hopefully by the end of the episode,

00:02:10.004 --> 00:02:14.520
our listeners will think that
the correct decision is obvious.

00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:18.963
This might sound like a tall order, but
you'd be surprised how often people do

00:02:18.963 --> 00:02:22.817
things in business that are obviously
wrong without realizing it.

00:02:22.817 --> 00:02:26.748
&gt;&gt; Well, I certainly have seen a number of
leaders make mistakes and what I find so

00:02:26.748 --> 00:02:30.444
wonderful about your podcast is that
you actually get people to come in and

00:02:30.444 --> 00:02:31.896
talk about their mistakes.

00:02:31.896 --> 00:02:35.230
And you help others avoid those mistakes
and learn how to make better ones.

00:02:35.230 --> 00:02:37.898
So I have listened and
I think it's fantastic, and

00:02:37.898 --> 00:02:40.020
I encourage everybody to listen in.

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:44.939
Jules given that you both are focused on
helping people make better decisions, can

00:02:44.939 --> 00:02:50.040
you share a specific idea or lesson you've
learned that can help us in this area?

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:54.404
&gt;&gt; So I think that when making a decision
people often have a mental model of

00:02:54.404 --> 00:02:59.180
the world that is static or fixed but
the world just doesn't work that way.

00:02:59.180 --> 00:03:03.656
It's highly dynamic and when I say that, I
don't just mean that changes happen around

00:03:03.656 --> 00:03:06.310
us continuously that we
have to somehow deal with.

00:03:06.310 --> 00:03:10.687
What I mean is, when you move a piece on
your chess board, the whole game changes,

00:03:10.687 --> 00:03:14.874
and the rest of the world who's on the
other side of the chessboard will respond

00:03:14.874 --> 00:03:16.910
to that move you just made.

00:03:16.910 --> 00:03:20.930
So there are two ways of thinking about
the chess game of life first, think about

00:03:20.930 --> 00:03:25.390
you being allowed to make the next ten
moves without the other side responding.

00:03:25.390 --> 00:03:28.640
You can win that game quite quickly and
it isn't all that hard.

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:32.290
But not to think about the other playing
responding after every move you make.

00:03:32.290 --> 00:03:34.340
This is a very different competitive game.

00:03:34.340 --> 00:03:35.974
It is much harder to play the game and

00:03:35.974 --> 00:03:39.370
you have to think very carefully
about the move that you want to make.

00:03:39.370 --> 00:03:42.181
That thought process can
be really frustrating, but

00:03:42.181 --> 00:03:45.920
ignoring those dynamic effects
doesn't really get you anywhere.

00:03:45.920 --> 00:03:48.650
It simply is the reality
we have to deal with.

00:03:48.650 --> 00:03:52.137
And because we think this mistake is so
common and important,

00:03:52.137 --> 00:03:54.760
we decided to call
the podcast All Else Equal.

00:03:54.760 --> 00:03:59.199
Because there are many statements that are
true when you can hold all else equal but

00:03:59.199 --> 00:04:01.590
in the real world,
all else is never equal.

00:04:01.590 --> 00:04:05.310
And then suddenly these statements no
longer hold and they can lead you astray.

00:04:05.310 --> 00:04:05.930
&gt;&gt; I get it.

00:04:05.930 --> 00:04:09.300
I was wondering where you got the name
of your podcast, but thank you.

00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:11.240
Well, I'm a lousy chess player.

00:04:11.240 --> 00:04:14.809
I totally understand what you mean and
I think the focus that you have in

00:04:14.809 --> 00:04:17.940
the approach you take can
be really helpful for.

00:04:17.940 --> 00:04:21.464
I'd like to follow up though Jules,
what advice do you have on how

00:04:21.464 --> 00:04:24.870
we actually can go about
communicating the mistakes we make?

00:04:24.870 --> 00:04:28.960
&gt;&gt; Well let's start by saying it's really
hard, I think we can both agree on that.

00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:29.720
&gt;&gt; Absolutely.

00:04:29.720 --> 00:04:33.695
&gt;&gt; The most important thing you need
to do before communicating a mistake is

00:04:33.695 --> 00:04:36.370
thinking through what happened carefully.

00:04:36.370 --> 00:04:40.400
What in business language I think is
often called do a root cause analysis.

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:44.030
Why did that mistake happen and
what can I learn from that?

00:04:44.030 --> 00:04:48.319
Because admitting mistakes is hard enough
as it is and many people will try to avoid

00:04:48.319 --> 00:04:51.740
doing so because they experience
it as a humiliating thing.

00:04:51.740 --> 00:04:54.498
If you go to your manager and
simply admit the mistake and

00:04:54.498 --> 00:04:58.093
then your manager asks can you help
me understand what happened and why?

00:04:58.093 --> 00:05:02.186
You better have a good answer if you have
the explanation as well as your plan for

00:05:02.186 --> 00:05:03.872
the future ready at that point.

00:05:03.872 --> 00:05:06.714
You can actually come out of
the conversation stronger and

00:05:06.714 --> 00:05:10.079
you can turn it into a positive
experience that isn't humiliating.

00:05:10.079 --> 00:05:13.186
&gt;&gt; So it sounds to me like two
things that are important there,

00:05:13.186 --> 00:05:15.744
one is approached mistakes
as part of learning.

00:05:15.744 --> 00:05:19.048
There's value to be taken from
the mistakes, so you can avoid it or

00:05:19.048 --> 00:05:20.646
do things better in the future.

00:05:20.646 --> 00:05:23.771
And then do that root cause
analysis to really understand, so

00:05:23.771 --> 00:05:26.730
instead of avoiding and
running from it or hiding from it.

00:05:26.730 --> 00:05:29.199
I don't know if embraces the right
word but at least accepted and

00:05:29.199 --> 00:05:30.491
then figure out what went wrong.

00:05:30.491 --> 00:05:31.950
Is that what I heard you say?

00:05:31.950 --> 00:05:34.060
&gt;&gt; Absolutely, but
what the second thing is,

00:05:34.060 --> 00:05:37.480
don't follow the first mistake with
the second mistake, do not come to

00:05:37.480 --> 00:05:41.378
the meeting in which you're supposed
to explain the first mistake prepared,.

00:05:41.378 --> 00:05:45.510
Be prepared so that you've done the
analysis before going into that meeting.

00:05:45.510 --> 00:05:49.197
So that you can immediately give it
a positive twist as opposed to just

00:05:49.197 --> 00:05:52.007
being a bad experience for
you in which you feel bad.

00:05:52.007 --> 00:05:55.457
&gt;&gt; Well, that echoes a lesson we've
talked a lot about on this podcast,

00:05:55.457 --> 00:05:58.180
which is take the time to
prepare it can really help.

00:05:58.180 --> 00:06:00.710
Jonathan, I'd like to turn
to you allow me to ask

00:06:00.710 --> 00:06:04.291
you the same question I asked Jules,
can you share a best practice for

00:06:04.291 --> 00:06:07.746
better decision making,
&gt;&gt; Well he called the podcast or

00:06:07.746 --> 00:06:10.795
else equal because he wanted
to highlight a common and

00:06:10.795 --> 00:06:14.550
costly mistake that comes up
in many business contexts.

00:06:14.550 --> 00:06:15.973
When you make a decision,

00:06:15.973 --> 00:06:20.440
you have to take into account the effect
of that decision on the world.

00:06:20.440 --> 00:06:22.440
Too often people ignore that.

00:06:22.440 --> 00:06:25.272
They engage what I would call
all else equal thinking And

00:06:25.272 --> 00:06:29.400
then evaluate the effect of the decision
holding everything else the same.

00:06:29.400 --> 00:06:31.800
You can never do that.

00:06:31.800 --> 00:06:34.908
I know it sounds obvious
as I talk about it, but

00:06:34.908 --> 00:06:40.270
you'd be surprised how often in the
context of a decision, people get fooled.

00:06:41.320 --> 00:06:43.867
We're going to explore many of
those contexts on the show.

00:06:43.867 --> 00:06:47.962
&gt;&gt; I think that is so important to
understand that the environment is dynamic

00:06:47.962 --> 00:06:51.593
and you need to be able to adjust and
adapt to what's happening and

00:06:51.593 --> 00:06:53.060
not have fixed thinking.

00:06:53.060 --> 00:06:57.693
So clearly something that people
can learn from you in your podcast.

00:06:57.693 --> 00:07:02.766
When I coach non finance executives and
senior leaders they often dread and

00:07:02.766 --> 00:07:06.310
perhaps even fear their
interactions with those and

00:07:06.310 --> 00:07:08.661
finance like the CFO for example.

00:07:08.661 --> 00:07:12.136
Because they often get
their proposals rejected or

00:07:12.136 --> 00:07:16.496
feel that they need to do battle
I've heard you talk about this as

00:07:16.496 --> 00:07:21.110
a communication challenge in
terms of framing and language.

00:07:21.110 --> 00:07:24.160
Can you share your thinking on
this type of communication?

00:07:24.160 --> 00:07:24.900
&gt;&gt; Yes, absolutely.

00:07:24.900 --> 00:07:28.847
So I think the first thing to establish
is, we think through language.

00:07:28.847 --> 00:07:33.910
If we don't speak the same language,
it's very difficult to align our thoughts.

00:07:33.910 --> 00:07:35.400
So let's start with that.

00:07:35.400 --> 00:07:36.634
So and for that reason,

00:07:36.634 --> 00:07:39.859
I think that one of the reasons
why regardless of their major.

00:07:39.859 --> 00:07:43.855
A business school student should learn
finance and some of the math that comes

00:07:43.855 --> 00:07:48.760
with it is that you learn the language
of quantitative dynamic decision making.

00:07:48.760 --> 00:07:52.629
How do you know whether something is
a good decision unless you have properly

00:07:52.629 --> 00:07:55.772
traded off the upsides and
the downsides or what economists or

00:07:55.772 --> 00:07:58.435
finance people call benefits and costs?

00:07:58.435 --> 00:08:01.407
The discipline that finance brings
to the table really helps with that,

00:08:01.407 --> 00:08:05.060
particularly when the costs and
the benefits happen at different times.

00:08:05.060 --> 00:08:08.712
So I'm not saying that everybody has to
learn all the nitty gritty details of

00:08:08.712 --> 00:08:12.135
finance, but you do need to understand
at least at a conceptual level,

00:08:12.135 --> 00:08:15.958
what goes into making a good decision and
why quantities are important in that,

00:08:15.958 --> 00:08:17.638
not just qualitative arguments.

00:08:17.638 --> 00:08:21.403
Now, on the other side, and that's the
other side of the communication challenge,

00:08:21.403 --> 00:08:24.283
I think it's important for
quantitatively-oriented people to

00:08:24.283 --> 00:08:26.393
translate their insights
into plain English.

00:08:26.393 --> 00:08:31.026
It's easy to make finance sound completely
incomprehensible by using tons of jargon

00:08:31.026 --> 00:08:34.838
and acronyms, which is really
unnecessary as far as I'm concerned.

00:08:34.838 --> 00:08:39.228
I think a very large part of teaching,
say, the core finance class,

00:08:39.228 --> 00:08:42.480
is to make people
familiar with the jargon.

00:08:42.480 --> 00:08:46.282
And really show them that once you
translate this jargon into plain English,

00:08:46.282 --> 00:08:48.691
most of these concepts
are really quite simple.

00:08:48.691 --> 00:08:50.932
&gt;&gt; That's so powerful what you said.

00:08:50.932 --> 00:08:54.711
I mean, both sides of the interaction
in any communication need to

00:08:54.711 --> 00:08:56.202
speak the same language.

00:08:56.202 --> 00:08:57.788
We need to avoid jargon,

00:08:57.788 --> 00:09:02.327
we need to make sure that the terms
are something that both understand.

00:09:02.327 --> 00:09:06.228
And we have to take time to learn
the perspective of the other,

00:09:06.228 --> 00:09:08.140
what's important to them.

00:09:08.140 --> 00:09:12.228
I think it's fantastic that two professors
of finance have said, you know what,

00:09:12.228 --> 00:09:15.603
we need to speak just in plain
English to help people understand, so

00:09:15.603 --> 00:09:16.910
thank you for that.

00:09:16.910 --> 00:09:17.550
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:09:17.550 --> 00:09:21.287
&gt;&gt; Now speaking about how we
think about things, Jonathan,

00:09:21.287 --> 00:09:25.266
I know you have spent a lot of
time thinking about how we think.

00:09:25.266 --> 00:09:30.540
And even further, how we go about
teaching others how to think better.

00:09:30.540 --> 00:09:34.788
What thoughts do you have on this
particular topic that you can share?

00:09:34.788 --> 00:09:38.410
&gt;&gt; Well, this is going to be an important
theme of the podcast series.

00:09:38.410 --> 00:09:41.314
It's pretty easy to talk
about best practices, but

00:09:41.314 --> 00:09:45.012
putting them into practice is
really another matter completely.

00:09:45.012 --> 00:09:45.785
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:09:45.785 --> 00:09:47.737
&gt;&gt; We are emotional creatures and

00:09:47.737 --> 00:09:51.269
our emotions materially
affect how we make decisions.

00:09:51.269 --> 00:09:54.505
For example,
we learn best when we make a mistake and

00:09:54.505 --> 00:09:57.386
then recognize that we
have made the mistake.

00:09:57.386 --> 00:10:02.510
But for most of us, it's really difficult
to admit that we made a mistake.

00:10:02.510 --> 00:10:06.377
So an important part of teaching is
understanding that emotions often stand in

00:10:06.377 --> 00:10:07.440
the way of learning.

00:10:08.460 --> 00:10:10.546
Of course, I do this myself all the time.

00:10:10.546 --> 00:10:14.445
That is, I let my emotions get in
the way of learning and growing.

00:10:14.445 --> 00:10:17.640
Now, the best way to teach
something is by modeling.

00:10:17.640 --> 00:10:22.662
So part of teaching is making
sure to model the behavior.

00:10:22.662 --> 00:10:25.518
So admitting that when
you make a mistake and

00:10:25.518 --> 00:10:29.389
showing students how you can
learn from your own mistakes.

00:10:29.389 --> 00:10:34.188
When I do make a mistake in the classroom,
I'm always very careful,

00:10:34.188 --> 00:10:40.095
I try to be very careful, to be completely
upfront about the mistake, how I made it.

00:10:40.095 --> 00:10:45.139
So students can see nobody
knows the material perfectly.

00:10:45.139 --> 00:10:48.675
And it's through the mistakes that you
really get this deep understanding.

00:10:48.675 --> 00:10:51.788
&gt;&gt; I think the notion of how
emotions affect us in almost

00:10:51.788 --> 00:10:56.840
everything we do is critical, especially
when it comes to the way we think.

00:10:56.840 --> 00:10:59.838
And modeling is so important,
and the willingness.

00:10:59.838 --> 00:11:03.161
And I strive to do the same thing you do.

00:11:03.161 --> 00:11:08.117
That when a mistake is made,
to acknowledge it, to learn from it, and

00:11:08.117 --> 00:11:10.890
to help others feel more comfortable.

00:11:10.890 --> 00:11:14.007
Not only taking the risks they need
that might lead to mistakes, but

00:11:14.007 --> 00:11:17.023
when they make the mistake,
to acknowledge it and learn from it.

00:11:17.023 --> 00:11:19.367
So that's really important information.

00:11:19.367 --> 00:11:22.753
And I think can help us all in
not only our decision making, but

00:11:22.753 --> 00:11:24.396
in most of our interactions.

00:11:24.396 --> 00:11:29.238
Given the wide variety of topics you both
teach and study, do you have any last

00:11:29.238 --> 00:11:33.505
best practices, advice or
frameworks you'd like to impart to us?

00:11:33.505 --> 00:11:37.799
&gt;&gt; Well, I have strong views
about teaching and education.

00:11:37.799 --> 00:11:42.788
I think that the way we go about educating
is really an historical accident that was

00:11:42.788 --> 00:11:47.144
constrained by the technology that
we had for most of human existence.

00:11:47.144 --> 00:11:51.778
But the fact is people do not learn at the
same pace, nor do they learn the same way.

00:11:51.778 --> 00:11:55.748
So our traditional way of learning where
we teach everybody at the same pace and

00:11:55.748 --> 00:11:58.287
the same way,
I don't think it's that helpful.

00:11:58.287 --> 00:12:01.134
I also don't think we learn
by being lectured to.

00:12:01.134 --> 00:12:03.437
Really, the way human
beings learn is by doing.

00:12:03.437 --> 00:12:07.680
So I changed the style that I
taught about a decade ago, and

00:12:07.680 --> 00:12:12.719
I guess the colloquial name for
how I teach is the flipped classroom.

00:12:12.719 --> 00:12:16.970
Students do the homework and
read the book before the lecture.

00:12:16.970 --> 00:12:20.765
And in the lecture,
we practice by working problems.

00:12:20.765 --> 00:12:23.562
So I'm trying to teach by doing.

00:12:23.562 --> 00:12:28.730
&gt;&gt; This notion of flipping the classroom,
where people do the work in advance so

00:12:28.730 --> 00:12:33.991
you can actually engage in the material
in person is a great tool for teaching.

00:12:33.991 --> 00:12:37.456
But I would also argue in business
settings and business meetings,

00:12:37.456 --> 00:12:38.748
the same thing applies.

00:12:38.748 --> 00:12:42.534
Instead of doing updates,
where all you do is recount information,

00:12:42.534 --> 00:12:45.339
have people do that on their
own time in advance, so

00:12:45.339 --> 00:12:49.149
when you come together you can
collaborate, discuss, and decide.

00:12:49.149 --> 00:12:53.478
So the notion of a flipped classroom,
which I think is very powerful for

00:12:53.478 --> 00:12:57.388
education, I think also applies
in the business world as well.

00:12:57.388 --> 00:13:02.563
&gt;&gt; One way to learn
the quickest is by teaching.

00:13:02.563 --> 00:13:05.325
And so you can take the flipped
classroom even one step further.

00:13:05.325 --> 00:13:08.036
So in the last semester that I taught,

00:13:08.036 --> 00:13:13.817
what I did was on the last class I had the
students teach the material to the class.

00:13:13.817 --> 00:13:14.491
&gt;&gt; Brilliant.

00:13:14.491 --> 00:13:18.368
&gt;&gt; So in that way they had to formulate
in their own words what they thought

00:13:18.368 --> 00:13:22.960
the most important things were that
they learned throughout the semester.

00:13:22.960 --> 00:13:24.780
And I had a very good experience with it,

00:13:24.780 --> 00:13:25.818
I would recommend it
&gt;&gt; So

00:13:25.818 --> 00:13:28.399
there's actually a teaching technique,
Jules,

00:13:28.399 --> 00:13:32.612
that is based on what you just said, it's
called a jigsaw, after a jigsaw puzzle.

00:13:32.612 --> 00:13:36.333
Where you actually empower
people to teach each other.

00:13:36.333 --> 00:13:39.036
In other words, they're each putting
a piece together of the puzzle, so

00:13:39.036 --> 00:13:40.355
everybody learns from each other.

00:13:40.355 --> 00:13:43.787
And you're right, I mean,
I've experienced this in my own life.

00:13:43.787 --> 00:13:47.450
You really learn something when you have
to teach somebody else how to do it or

00:13:47.450 --> 00:13:50.098
how to think that way and
empowering others to do that.

00:13:50.098 --> 00:13:54.168
And again, not just in an academic
classroom setting, I think this applies to

00:13:54.168 --> 00:13:57.698
how we run meetings, how we run
teams in the business world as well.

00:13:57.698 --> 00:14:00.309
So thank you for that insight.

00:14:00.309 --> 00:14:04.662
Now, I know your podcast ends a little
different than mine, but I'm hoping you're

00:14:04.662 --> 00:14:08.915
willing to answer the same three questions
that I ask every guest on this podcast.

00:14:08.915 --> 00:14:09.914
Are you guys up for that?

00:14:09.914 --> 00:14:10.607
&gt;&gt; Of course.

00:14:10.607 --> 00:14:15.674
&gt;&gt; So Jonathan, If you were to
capture the best communication advice

00:14:15.674 --> 00:14:20.830
you ever received as a five to seven
word presentation slide title,

00:14:20.830 --> 00:14:22.470
what would it be?

00:14:22.470 --> 00:14:25.036
&gt;&gt; Keep things simple.

00:14:25.036 --> 00:14:27.115
&gt;&gt; I love it, and
I love how simple that was.

00:14:27.115 --> 00:14:30.624
You only used three words, I gave you
five to seven and you only used three.

00:14:30.624 --> 00:14:33.537
Talk to me a little bit more of
why simplicity is so important.

00:14:33.537 --> 00:14:38.434
&gt;&gt; First of all, we say all complicated
concepts actually have a simple core.

00:14:38.434 --> 00:14:40.674
And if they don't,
there's probably something wrong.

00:14:40.674 --> 00:14:44.063
But the other thing is people
don't learn complicated concepts.

00:14:44.063 --> 00:14:45.310
It's too difficult.

00:14:45.310 --> 00:14:46.862
We only learn simple stuff.

00:14:46.862 --> 00:14:49.430
So if you think you're
going to get up there and

00:14:49.430 --> 00:14:52.486
explain something very
complicated to somebody and

00:14:52.486 --> 00:14:56.872
they're going to walk away and
learn it, you're just fooling yourself.

00:14:56.872 --> 00:14:59.214
And obviously simple is relative.

00:14:59.214 --> 00:15:03.613
What's simple to a theoretical
physicist isn't simple to me, but

00:15:03.613 --> 00:15:06.479
at every level,
you have to keep it simple.

00:15:06.479 --> 00:15:10.641
&gt;&gt; If I can add to that, so
I think it's important also for

00:15:10.641 --> 00:15:14.818
groups to set a culture where
simplicity is rewarded.

00:15:14.818 --> 00:15:18.647
I think that there are certain settings
where over complicating things,

00:15:18.647 --> 00:15:22.734
people think that it makes them sound
intelligent and makes them look better.

00:15:22.734 --> 00:15:25.986
But I think in the end,
nobody is helped with that approach.

00:15:25.986 --> 00:15:29.761
&gt;&gt; I absolutely appreciate
what you both are saying, and

00:15:29.761 --> 00:15:33.464
I think it's really cool
that you think this way too.

00:15:33.464 --> 00:15:37.001
Because if you can simplify something,
and to me,

00:15:37.001 --> 00:15:41.128
I like to use the word accessible
maybe more than simplify.

00:15:41.128 --> 00:15:45.176
But if you can make something accessible,
take it down to the the most basic

00:15:45.176 --> 00:15:47.695
elements, you can really
help people learn.

00:15:47.695 --> 00:15:50.285
And what it require from your perspective,

00:15:50.285 --> 00:15:54.970
is you really have to think through
what the other person knows and needs.

00:15:54.970 --> 00:15:58.042
In order to get it to that simplified,
accessible point.

00:15:58.042 --> 00:16:01.256
So, it's not just about saying
it as simply as possible,

00:16:01.256 --> 00:16:05.400
it's really about the thought process
beforehand that helps you do that.

00:16:05.400 --> 00:16:08.140
So I love, keep it simple.

00:16:08.140 --> 00:16:12.646
Now let me ask both of you this question,
who is a communicator that you admire,

00:16:12.646 --> 00:16:13.350
and why?

00:16:13.350 --> 00:16:15.240
And Joules, why don't you go first.

00:16:15.240 --> 00:16:17.475
&gt;&gt; My favorite communicator
is Keith Jarrett,

00:16:17.475 --> 00:16:20.320
who is one of the best jazz
pianists in the world.

00:16:20.320 --> 00:16:23.490
And he doesn't even need words to do it.

00:16:23.490 --> 00:16:25.614
I think that there
are things that music and

00:16:25.614 --> 00:16:30.350
particularly improvisation can communicate
that no other language can communicate.

00:16:30.350 --> 00:16:32.540
&gt;&gt; I am a huge fan of improvisation.

00:16:32.540 --> 00:16:35.350
We've spent a few episodes
talking to experts on improv.

00:16:35.350 --> 00:16:39.904
And I think you're right, those who have
the ability to put things together in

00:16:39.904 --> 00:16:43.425
the moment can communicate in
ways that are just fantastic,

00:16:43.425 --> 00:16:45.441
to participate in as an audience.

00:16:45.441 --> 00:16:49.230
And be it spoken or
playing the piano, it's fantastic.

00:16:49.230 --> 00:16:52.190
Jonathan, I'm curious,
who do you admire and why?

00:16:52.190 --> 00:16:55.240
&gt;&gt; Well, I mean,
I wish I had as great as Joules.

00:16:55.240 --> 00:16:59.510
I mean, I think Michael Lewis
is a phenomenal communicator.

00:16:59.510 --> 00:17:04.276
I think he can take pretty complicated
concepts and make them very simple and

00:17:04.276 --> 00:17:09.231
communicate them very effectively
&gt;&gt; Joules, our final question for you.

00:17:09.231 --> 00:17:14.443
What are the first three ingredients that
go into a successful communication recipe?

00:17:14.443 --> 00:17:19.260
&gt;&gt; So I would say the first ingredient
to effective communication is listening.

00:17:19.260 --> 00:17:23.215
So unless you have at least some basic
understanding what the person you're

00:17:23.215 --> 00:17:27.232
talking to is thinking or often,
particularly in the educational setting,

00:17:27.232 --> 00:17:31.110
struggling with, what you're going
to say is not going to arrive.

00:17:31.110 --> 00:17:34.820
So you first have to figure out what is
going on in the head of the other person.

00:17:34.820 --> 00:17:36.050
So that's ingredient one.

00:17:36.050 --> 00:17:39.336
I would say the second ingredient
is the one we just mentioned,

00:17:39.336 --> 00:17:41.100
which is simplicity.

00:17:41.100 --> 00:17:45.126
I think that there are certain academic
fields that seem to go out of their way to

00:17:45.126 --> 00:17:48.030
say things in the most
complicated way possible.

00:17:48.030 --> 00:17:51.322
And I think this just serves
as a barrier to entry for

00:17:51.322 --> 00:17:53.936
people not belonging to the in-crowd.

00:17:53.936 --> 00:17:57.433
But in the end it doesn't help either
the people in the out-crowd but

00:17:57.433 --> 00:18:00.155
also even the people in
the in-crowd are not helped.

00:18:00.155 --> 00:18:03.650
Because if you want to get new ideas,
new ideas are very rare.

00:18:03.650 --> 00:18:07.792
And to get new ideas, you need a lot of
people to engage in the conversation,

00:18:07.792 --> 00:18:09.690
particularly qualified people.

00:18:09.690 --> 00:18:13.351
So why would you put barriers to entry
particularly in language to keep

00:18:13.351 --> 00:18:14.080
people out?

00:18:14.080 --> 00:18:15.460
I don't think that's effective.

00:18:15.460 --> 00:18:20.258
And then finally, the third ingredient I
would say is also again in an educational

00:18:20.258 --> 00:18:22.200
setting is humility.

00:18:22.200 --> 00:18:24.679
And I, what I mean with that is,
in the end,

00:18:24.679 --> 00:18:28.181
I think it's important to take
yourself out of the equation.

00:18:28.181 --> 00:18:31.838
In the sense that in the end,
the only measure of success that you

00:18:31.838 --> 00:18:35.835
have as an educator is how much more
the students know or have learned is

00:18:35.835 --> 00:18:40.560
even a better way than just knowing,
have learned at the end of your course.

00:18:40.560 --> 00:18:42.120
The rest is irrelevant.

00:18:42.120 --> 00:18:46.306
&gt;&gt; The idea of listening and
being clear, accessible and

00:18:46.306 --> 00:18:49.490
simple, very, very important.

00:18:49.490 --> 00:18:53.880
But the last point about humility,
I think is fundamental.

00:18:53.880 --> 00:18:58.095
And not only is it an education, but
just I think in all communication,

00:18:58.095 --> 00:18:59.830
it's not about you.

00:18:59.830 --> 00:19:03.232
It's about the people you're speaking to
and what they can do with the information

00:19:03.232 --> 00:19:06.750
you're providing and there's that
understanding is really critical.

00:19:06.750 --> 00:19:10.220
Jonathan and Jules, I knew this
was going to be fun and exciting.

00:19:10.220 --> 00:19:11.410
Thank you so much.

00:19:11.410 --> 00:19:14.440
You've provided us with so
many insights in so

00:19:14.440 --> 00:19:17.790
many ideas to help us
make better decisions.

00:19:17.790 --> 00:19:23.290
I encourage everyone listening to listen
into their podcast, all else equal.

00:19:23.290 --> 00:19:26.080
Best of luck to both of you and
congratulations.

00:19:26.080 --> 00:19:27.180
&gt;&gt; Thanks so much, man.

00:19:27.180 --> 00:19:28.320
&gt;&gt; Thank you very much, man.

00:19:28.320 --> 00:19:29.243
It's been a lot of fun.

